CLASS:: PhaseLock
SUMMARY:: A bank of phase-locked oscillators driven by an external master phase
CATEGORIES:: UGens>Generators, UGens>Triggers
RELATED:: Classes/BlipSync, Classes/Phasor

DESCRIPTION::

STRONG::n:: phase oscillators, each with its own natural frequency, each pulled
toward an external master phase at a rational ratio. The output is STRONG::n::
phase ramps in 0..1 — the same thing link::Classes/BlipSync:: reads with
code::freq: 0:: and code::track: 1::.

The reason the master is a SIGNAL rather than a frequency is that banks can then
be CHAINED. One bank's output phase becomes the master of a bank below it, so a
hierarchy is a tree of these — one instance per node, each with its own coupling
strength and ratios. A PLL that generates its own master internally can only
ever be one level deep.

Per sample, for slave STRONG::j:::

TELETYPE::
    e      = wrap(ratios[j]*master - subs[j]*ph[j])        // in [-0.5, 0.5)
    ph[j] += freq[j]/sr
           + (k/subs[j]) * e                               // toward the parent
           + mutual * R*sin(2pi*(psi - ph[j]))             // toward the siblings
::

code::k:: and code::mutual:: are both in STRONG::Hz::, and both mean the same
thing: the largest frequency pull that term can exert. For code::k:: that is
also the capture range — a slave detuned from its locked frequency by less than
code::k/subs:: Hz locks, one detuned by more slips. Being frequencies they are
sample rate independent; the identical run at 44.1, 48 and 96 kHz gives the same
lock. The loop settles with a time constant of code::1/(2*k):: seconds.

Measured, four slaves at 2:3:4:5 against a 25 Hz conductor, detuned by
+3/−4/+5/−2 Hz (phase lock over time, 1.0 = the offset holds):

TELETYPE::
    k (Hz)     x2      x3      x4      x5
       0     0.018   0.022   0.000   0.028
       1     0.129   0.061   0.054   0.187
       2.5   0.347   0.242   0.190   1.000     <- captures the 2 Hz one
       4.5   1.000   1.000   0.385   1.000     <- and the 3 and 4, not the 5
      10     1.000   1.000   1.000   1.000
::

NOTE::
Give the slaves different code::iphases::. A bank started with every phase at 0
is a degenerate state: with any coupling at all they simply stay there.
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: ar

ARGUMENT:: master
The master phase, 0..1. A link::Classes/Phasor::, another PhaseLock output, a
link::Classes/BlipSync:: phase output. Audio or control rate; a control-rate
master is interpolated the short way round, so the block containing the wrap
does not sweep backwards through the whole cycle.

ARGUMENT:: freqs
Array of natural frequencies, one per slave. Its size sets STRONG::n::. Audio or
control rate.

ARGUMENT:: k
Pull toward the master, in Hz — see above. 0 leaves the slaves free running;
negative pushes away from the master instead. Audio or control rate.

ARGUMENT:: mutual
Pull toward the bank's own mean field, in Hz. Makes the slaves cohere with each
other as well as with the master, and a bank with no master at all is then a
Kuramoto bank. Negative repels them into anti-phase. 0 skips the computation.
Measured on eight oscillators spread over 90–120 Hz, mean field coherence R:

TELETYPE::
    mutual   0 Hz    5 Hz   12 Hz   25 Hz   60 Hz   -30 Hz
    R        0.230   0.280   0.292   0.892   0.986   0.168
::

The transition sits where the coupling passes the width of the bank, and the
negative value drives R below its uncoupled value.

ARGUMENT:: ratios
Numerator of the lock ratio: STRONG::subs:: slave cycles per STRONG::ratios::
master cycles. code::ratios: 3:: is three pulses per master cycle.
Wrap-extended to STRONG::n::. Audio or control rate.

Rounded to an integer internally, and that is not a limitation but the
definition: code::theta -> n*theta:: is a well defined map of the circle only
for integer code::n::, and a fractional one puts a jump of code::frac(n):: into
the lock target at every wrap of the master — a periodic kick, not a lock.
Because it rounds, it can be MODULATED: an LFO on it steps cleanly from one lock
ratio to the next instead of sliding through undefined ones. Give
STRONG::freqs:: the same modulator so each slave already sits near its new
target; otherwise the jump has to fall inside the capture range, which needs a
much stiffer loop.

ARGUMENT:: subs
Denominator of the lock ratio, rounded and clamped to >= 1. code::subs: 3:: is
one pulse per three master cycles; code::ratios: 3, subs: 2:: is a three
against two. Wrap-extended to STRONG::n::. Audio or control rate.

ARGUMENT:: iphases
Per-slave initial phase in cycles. Wrap-extended to STRONG::n::. INIT RATE.

EXAMPLES::

A graded set of complete patches — one level, rational ratios, a mutual bank,
two and three level hierarchies, modulated ratios and a full signal chain — is
in code::examples/phase-networks.scd:: in the source tree. A couple of them:

CODE::
s.boot;

// four voices at 2:3:4:5 against a 25 Hz conductor, each deliberately detuned.
// Mouse Y is the capture range in Hz: they lock one at a time as it passes
// each voice's detuning.
(
{
    var cf = 25, ratios = [2, 3, 4, 5], detune = [3, -4, 5, -2];
    var freqs = Array.fill(4, { |i| (cf * ratios[i]) + detune[i] });
    var master = Phasor.ar(0, cf * SampleDur.ir, 0, 1);
    var ph = PhaseLock.ar(master, freqs, MouseY.kr(0, 8), ratios: ratios,
                          iphases: (0..3) / 4);
    Splay.ar(ph.collect { |p|
        BlipSync.ar(0, 5000, phase: p, track: 1, tilt: -3).at(0)
    }) * 0.2
}.play
)

// two levels: a conductor, four group nodes ratio-locked to it, four voices
// locked under each node. Mouse X = pull inside a group, Y = pull to the top.
// Bottom right is the interesting corner: four rigid groups, drifting.
(
{
    var g = 4, m = 4, cf = 25;
    var groupF = [53, 71, 105, 123], ratios = [2, 3, 4, 5];
    var master = Phasor.ar(0, cf * SampleDur.ir, 0, 1);
    var gph = PhaseLock.ar(master, groupF, MouseY.kr(0, 8), ratios: ratios,
                           iphases: (0..g-1) / g);
    var leaves = Array.fill(g, { |q|
        PhaseLock.ar(gph[q], Array.fill(m, { |j| groupF[q] + (j * 1.3) - 1.95 }),
                     MouseX.kr(0, 30), iphases: ((0..m-1) / m) + 0.05)
    }).flatten;
    Splay.ar(leaves.collect { |p|
        BlipSync.ar(0, 6000, phase: p, track: 1, tilt: -2).at(0)
    }) * 0.2
}.play
)
::

STRONG::Notes::

LIST::
## A tree of banks is not the same thing as one mean field. In a tree each node
has a definite phase that its children follow, so a group holds together even
while its node drifts; in a mean field a group's phase is only the average of
its members and has no independent existence.
## The correction is proportional to the wrapped phase error, so it saturates at
half a cycle. Beyond the capture range the loop slips cycles rather than
diverging.
## code::mutual:: costs a sine and a cosine per slave per sample; leave it at 0
if the bank only follows a master.
## The bank is allocated at init time, so STRONG::n:: is fixed for the life of
the synth. Large trees need real time memory: raise code::s.options.memSize:: if
the post window reports an RTAlloc failure.
::
